body:
A parser registered for `application/json; charset=utf-8` is skipped when the client sends `charset=UTF-8`. The request silently falls back to the parser for the bare media type, or gets a 415 if there isn't one. This worked up to 5.7.1 (it's the case from #4583) and broke in 5.7.2 with the content-type rewrite in #6414.

`add()` lowercases the whole registration string, but `ContentType` keeps parameter values as sent, and `getParser` looks the parser up by `toString()`. So `charset="UTF-8"` no longer matches `charset="utf-8"`.

This lowercases only the `charset` value in the `ContentType` constructor, since charset names are case-insensitive (RFC 9110 8.3.2) and both registration and lookup go through it. Other parameter values are left alone, since they can be case-sensitive as discussed in #4583.

Repro:

```js
const app = Fastify()
app.addContentTypeParser('application/json; charset=utf-8', { parseAs: 'string' }, (req, body, done) => {
  done(null, { custom: true })
})
app.post('/', async (req) => req.body)

const res = await app.inject({
  method: 'POST',
  url: '/',
  payload: '{"x":1}',
  headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json; charset=UTF-8' }
})
// 5.7.1: {"custom":true}
// 5.7.2 and main: {"x":1}
```

#7022 and #6943 also touch `ContentType`, but only how values are serialized, so the code changes don't overlap.

Since it's a regression from 5.7.2, I think it's worth backporting to 5.x.

